(2. Your executable is a SQLite database) Tj
T*
(Source: Simon Willison's Weblog | Published: 2026-08-24T11:38:15+00:00) Tj
T*
(Link: https://simonwillison.net/2026/Aug/24/your-executable-is-a-sqlite-database/) Tj
T*
(Your executable is a SQLite database Farid Zakaria describes a neat Linux pattern for creating) Tj
T*
(a SQLite database file that can be directly used as an executable binary. The trick sets the) Tj
T*
(SQLite file format's 4-byte application ID \(68 bytes into the file\) to SELF, standing for) Tj
T*
(Structured Executable & Linkable Format. The various components of the ELF executable format) Tj
T*
(are then arranged into a number of different SQLite tables, using this schema . Their self-exec) Tj
T*
(interpreter \( C code here \) can then extract and execute the necessary pieces. You can) Tj
T*
(additionally use a Linux mechanism called binfmt_misc to teach the kernel to execute that any) Tj
T*
(time it encounters an executable matching that binary pattern. Farid uses NixOS here, but) Tj
T*
(without NixOS I think registration looks something like this: printf '%s\\n') Tj
T*
(':self:M:68:SELF::/usr/local/bin/self-exec:' \\ > /proc/sys/fs/binfmt_misc/register Via Hacker) Tj
T*
(News Tags: c , linux , sqlite) Tj
T*
() Tj
